(minutes. What actually happens on a busy wave is I walk down and read it off) Tj T*
(their sheet, otherwise we'd miss the slot.) Tj T*
([24:10] Priya Nair: My rule of thumb: if the forward hold looks heavy on the) Tj T*
(plan, I call the ramp before they start, not after. Nobody taught me that; I) Tj T*
(learned it after we had to re-trim three flights in one night.) Tj T*
([24:40] Interviewer: [M5.s1] Was that night more like following the procedure,) Tj T*
(needing an expert, working it out as you went, or firefighting?) Tj T*
([24:46] Priya Nair: Firefighting. It happens maybe once a month.) Tj T*
([27:15] Priya Nair: If the weight deviation is outside tolerance, it's the) Tj T*
(captain who decides, not us. The tolerance depends on the airline contract.) Tj T*
([29:02] Priya Nair: The captain either accepts or asks for a re-trim. Five to) Tj T*
(fifteen minutes.) Tj T*
([29:40] Interviewer: [M4.3] How do you decide what to propose when it's outside) Tj T*
(tolerance? Is there a rule for this, or does it depend?) Tj T*
([29:48] Priya Nair: There's no fixed answer. It depends on the captain, the) Tj T*
(weather, what's still on the belt. You read the situation.) Tj T*
([31:10] Priya Nair: Then I issue the final load sheet, around T-15.) Tj T*
ET
